module applied_sum_arm.neg_1

// A `match` ON AN APPLIED SUM NOVAC CANNOT SPELL -- refused BY NAME, and the
// name is the point.
//
// Until 2026-09-20 this said "this arm names a variant of a different sum than
// the value matched on". That sentence is about the PROGRAM and was a statement
// about the COMPILER: `Some` is registered under the generic `Option`, the
// scrutinee is an application, and the door compared type ids. The reader was
// sent looking for a sum mix-up that is not in their code.
//
// WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-10-01, AND WHY THE CELL MOVED OFF `Option`. An applied
// sum comes from the SHELL, with the oracle's tag names and value semantics,
// while novac emitted its own spelling (measured on `Option[int]`:
// `_novac_l2->tag` against a value struct, and an undeclared
// `NOVAC_TAG_..._Option_Some`). The wave match_applied_sum taught the emitter
// the shell's spelling for the two sums whose instances the shell names --
// `Option[T]` (`NovaOpt_<T>`) and `Result[T, E]` (`NovaRes_<T>_<E>`) -- so a
// match on `Option[int]` compiles now (novac/fixtures/match_applied_sum/).
//
// What stays refused is every OTHER applied sum the declarations hand over:
// the prelude's `Outcome[T]` is the one reachable today, and the shell spells
// it without a per-instance struct that novac could name. Letting it through
// the checker with the emitter unable to carry it is the silent-acceptance
// class this compiler keeps closing.
//
// A user's OWN generic sum never reaches this door: generic parameters are
// refused at the declaration (E2-b2). So this cell is about the shell's sums
// alone.

// NOVAC_EXPECT a `match` on an applied sum

fn finished_or_zero(o Outcome[int]) -> int => match o {
    Finished(v) => v
    Aborted => 0
}
